  <h1>India’s Water Crisis — Groundwater Depletion and Policy Challenges</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India, home to 18% of the world’s population but only 4% of global freshwater resources, is facing an acute water crisis. In 2024, The Hindu emphasized the twin challenges of groundwater depletion and poor water governance. With agriculture, urbanization, and climate change intensifying the stress, water security is emerging as a critical governance issue.
________________________________________
Scale of the Problem
•	Groundwater Dependence: India extracts ~250 billion cubic meters annually — the world’s highest.
•	Declining Tables: Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) notes that ~65% of wells show decline.
•	States at Risk: Punjab, Haryana, Rajasthan, and Tamil Nadu face alarming depletion.
•	Urban Stress: Cities like Bengaluru and Chennai projected to face “Day Zero” scenarios.
________________________________________
Drivers of Water Stress
1.	Agricultural Practices
o	MSP-driven monocultures of paddy and sugarcane in water-scarce regions.
o	Inefficient flood irrigation methods.
2.	Urbanization
o	Rapid urban growth outpacing supply.
o	Encroachment of lakes, wetlands, and recharge zones.
3.	Climate Change
o	Erratic monsoons, frequent droughts and floods.
o	Reduced recharge due to rising temperatures.
4.	Governance Deficits
o	Fragmented institutional responsibility.
o	Lack of water pricing and metering leads to over-extraction.
________________________________________
Government Initiatives
•	Jal Jeevan Mission (2019): Target to provide tap water to all rural households by 2024. Progress ~75% by end of 2024.
•	Atal Bhujal Yojana: Focused on community-led groundwater management in seven states.
•	Namami Gange: River rejuvenation with limited success on pollution control.
•	National Water Policy (2012): Called for integrated water resource management (IWRM).
________________________________________
Challenges
•	Implementation Gaps: Schemes uneven across states.
•	Community Participation: Limited in many water projects.
•	Pollution: Industrial effluents, untreated sewage add to scarcity.
•	Equity Concerns: Rural poor and marginalized often excluded from assured access.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-I (Geography): Water resources, climate change.
•	GS-II (Governance): Federalism and water-sharing disputes.
•	GS-III (Environment): Sustainable development, agriculture.
•	Essay:
o	“Water is the new oil of the 21st century.”
o	“Ensuring water security is India’s greatest governance challenge.”
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Agricultural Reforms: Crop diversification away from water-intensive crops; incentivize millets and pulses.<br/>2.	Efficient Irrigation: Expand drip and sprinkler systems under PMKSY.<br/>3.	Recharge & Conservation: Rainwater harvesting, watershed management.<br/>4.	Urban Water Management: Smart metering, wastewater recycling, lake restoration.<br/>5.	Institutional Strengthening: Update National Water Policy; empower water regulatory authorities.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s water crisis is not just ecological but a governance and social justice issue. Addressing it requires integrated reforms across agriculture, urban planning, and community participation. For UPSC aspirants, water policy offers an example of how resource management lies at the heart of sustainable development.</p>
